/opencode-src/bin/<REDACTED_HEX>/opencode", "run", "--print-logs", "-m", "anthropic/claude-opus-5-5", "--format", "json", "--title", "openbench", "Write a JSON file called /tmp/obench-cell-v4miy3_s/bench_opencode_regex-chess_0i4ql8j9/re.json that is a list of [regex, replacement] pairs. When executed in order with the Python code\n```\ndef all_legal_next_positions(fen):\n  for pattern, repl in json.load(open(\"/tmp/obench-cell-v4miy3_s/bench_opencode_regex-chess_0i4ql8j9/re.json\")):\n      fen = re.sub(pattern, repl, fen)\n  return fen.split(\"\\n\")\n```\nthis function should return the FEN position for all possible legal next chess positions.\n\nMake the following simplifying assumptions:\n- You will only be shown positions where it is white to move\n- Any promotions will only be made to Queen (assume that underpromotion is not a legal move)\n- You do not need to track the full-move or half-move count\n\nThe input will be provided, as a string like \"<REDACTED_B64> w kq - 0 1\".\nWhen run on this string, your answer must produce the following output:\n\"<REDACTED_B64> b kq - 0 0\\<REDACTED_B64> b kq - 0 0\\<REDACTED_B64> b kq - 0 0\"\nNote that the final output of the move and halfmove are allowed to be incorrect. So the above is scored properly even though the move numbers are wrong.\n\nWith these exceptions (and only these exceptions) you must implement a fully correct move generator, including:\n- Castling, with proper tracking of castling rights\n- Promotion, except only allow promotion to queen\n- En-passant\n\nThe length of re.json must be under 100,000 [regex, replacement]-pairs long, and under 10 megabytes in total.\n\nYou can look at the provided check.py to verify if your solution is correct. You will be tested on other positions as well.\n"], "completed": true, "concurrent_cells_at_end": 4, "concurrent_cells_at_start": 4, "dotnet_build_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 1, "embedded_version": "2.0.50", "ended_et": "2026-11-12T16:34:23.725436-05:00", "ended_on_rejection": false, "endpoint_location": "global", "endpoint_location_mixed": false, "env_cause": null, "env_failure": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "failure_subclass": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "stdout", "final_workspace_bytes": 481553, "final_workspace_skip_reason": null, "final_workspace_status": "saved", "final_workspace_tar": "/h2/drift-5140-1103/opus/5140/transcripts/with/opencode_regex-chess_claude-opus-5-5_trial2.workspace.tar.gz", "final_workspace_tar_bytes": 38036, "final_workspace_tar_sha256": "<REDACTED_HEX>", "first_after_build": false, "first_side": "with", "free_disk_gb": 37.96, "fresh_subagents": 0, "functional_pass": null, "gate_cell": false, "harness": "opencode", "harness_version": "0.0.0--202610170344 (/h1/opencode-src/bin/<REDACTED_HEX>/opencode)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "<HOST>", "image_digest": null, "image_scan_copies": [], "installed_anthropic": "2.0.50", "instance_id": "8187537343833955709", "instruction_app_rewrite": true, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "load_proof": "pass", "load_proof_basis": "user_agent", "load_proof_reason": "User-Agent 2.0.50", "loaded_anthropic": "2.0.50", "lock_wait_s": 0.0, "lockfile": "bun.lock", "lockfile_sha256": "<REDACTED_HEX>", "machine": "<HOST>", "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": null, "model_match_reason": null, "model_max_tokens": null, "network_mode": "local", "off_protocol": false, "opencode_cache": "/h1/opencode-src", "output_tail": "rook is captured\n  - en passant where the capturing pawn is pinned, including the case where both pawns leave the same rank\n  - promotions, both pushing and capturing\n\n**Two limitations:**\n- **No legal moves (checkmate or stalemate):** the function returns `[\"\"]` rather than an empty list. That's unavoidable, because `\"\".split(\"\\n\")` always gives `[\"\"]`. Positions taken from the moves of real games, like `check.py`'s, never hit this.\n- **Speed:** each position takes about 0.2 seconds, because every move rule is tried in turn.\n\n**How it works:**\n1. **Normalise:** the FEN is turned into a fixed-width line: 64 board squares (`.` for empty), 4 castling slots, and the en-passant file.\n2. **Generate moves:** there is one rule for each possible piece move (piece, from-square, to-square), with conditions that the squares in between are empty. When a rule's conditions hold on the original line, it adds the resulting position as a new line. The same approach covers double pushes (which set the en-passant file), promotion to queen only, en passant, and castling.\n3. **Castling through check:** a castling line temporarily puts extra white kings on the squares the king passes through. The normal \"king in check\" filter then rejects castling out of, through or into check, and afterwards those lines are converted to the real king and rook squares.\n4. **Remove illegal moves:** for each of the 64 squares, one rule deletes any line where a white king on that square is attacked by a black knight, pawn, king or sliding piece.\n5. **Castling rights:** each right is kept only if the king and that rook are still on their starting squares, which is the same rule python-chess applies. The en-passant square is always written after a double push; `check.py` accepts that even when python-chess shows `-`.\n6. **Output:** lines are converted back to FEN as `... b <castling> <ep> 0 0`.\n\n`gen.py` builds `re.json`, and `test.py` is the random-game test harness; `python3 test.py <seed> <games>` runs it.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "params_stripped": [], "params_stripped_requests": 0, "permission_rejections": 0, "pin_source": "override", "pin_source_class": "override", "probe_method": "embedded", "protocol_model": "claude-opus-5-5", "provider_loaded_mismatch": null, "provider_loaded_source": "embedded", "provider_lo